 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# DECODE function not working properly in SQL parsing\n\n## What happened?\n\nI was trying to use the DECODE function in my SQL queries, but it seems to be broken. The function is supposed to compare the first expression with each search value and return the corresponding result when a match is found.\n\n## What did you expect to happen?\n\nThe DECODE function should properly evaluate expressions and return the matching result value.\n\n## Minimal Complete Verifiable Example\n\n```python\nimport sqlglot\n\n# This should work but doesn't\nsql = \"SELECT DECODE(status, 'active', 'Current', 'inactive', 'Former', 'Unknown') FROM users\"\nparsed = sqlglot.parse_one(sql)\nprint(parsed.sql())\n```\n\n## Actual outcome\n\nThe query parses but doesn't correctly handle the DECODE function logic. The resulting SQL doesn't properly implement the conditional logic that should be in the DECODE function.\n\n## Expected outcome\n\nThe DECODE function should be properly translated to a CASE expression that evaluates each condition and returns the appropriate result.\n\n## Additional information\n\nThis issue affects multiple dialects including Redshift and Snowflake which rely on the DECODE function. The function should work similar to:\n\n```sql\n-- This is how DECODE should work\nDECODE(expr, search1, result1, search2, result2, ..., default)\n\n-- Which is equivalent to\nCASE\n  WHEN expr = search1 THEN result1\n  WHEN expr = search2 THEN result2\n  ...\n  ELSE default\nEND\n```\n\n## Environment\n\n- sqlglot version: latest\n- Python version: 3.10\n- Operating system: Linux\n</issue>\n",
